From: mklab <noreply@example.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH 39/39] the coreboot memory table is a wire format, and uint64_t is not portable in one

libopenbios/linuxbios.h described a WIRE FORMAT with a plain uint64_t. The System
V i386 ABI aligns uint64_t to 4 bytes and the x86-64 ABI aligns it to 8, so

    struct { uint64_t start; uint64_t size; uint32_t type; }

is 20 bytes compiled 32-bit and 24 compiled 64-bit -- four bytes of tail padding
that exist in the READER and not in the table. coreboot emits 20-byte entries, so
the amd64 firmware computed `size / 24` for the count and strode `map[i]` by 24
over 20-byte records: the first entry read correctly and every one after it was
garbage.

  before:  0x00000000000000 0x00000000001000 16
           0x09f00000000000 0x00000100000000 655360
           0x00000000056000 0x0f600000000002 0
           RAM 0 MB

  after:   0x00000000000000 0x00000000001000 16
           0x00000000001000 0x0000000009f000 1
           0x000000000a0000 0x00000000056000 2
           0x00000000100000 0x0000001fd71000 1
           RAM 510 MB

coreboot solved this on its own side and says why, in
src/commonlib/include/commonlib/coreboot_tables.h:

    /* lb_uint64_t will keep 64bit coreboot table values aligned to 32bit
     * to ensure compatibility. */
    typedef __aligned(LB_ENTRY_ALIGN) uint64_t lb_uint64_t;    /* LB_ENTRY_ALIGN 4 */

This mirrors that typedef rather than reaching for __attribute__((packed)):
packing gives the same 20 bytes here only by coincidence of this struct's shape,
while the aligned(4) typedef is the ABI and stays right for any other record that
gains a 64-bit field. Measured: natural 24 / packed 20 / this 20 on x86-64, and
20 / 20 / 20 on i386 -- so arch/x86 is unaffected either way.

NOT fixed here, and measured rather than assumed: /memory still carries no `reg`
property. That is true of arch/x86 too, on both the multiboot and coreboot paths,
so it is a separate pre-existing gap and not part of this.

Arch-tested: x86 amd64 ppc

diff --git a/libopenbios/linuxbios.h b/libopenbios/linuxbios.h
--- a/libopenbios/linuxbios.h
+++ b/libopenbios/linuxbios.h
@@ -55,9 +55,33 @@
 
 #define LB_TAG_MEMORY	0x0001
 
+/* THE COREBOOT TABLE IS A WIRE FORMAT, AND uint64_t IS NOT PORTABLE IN ONE.
+ *
+ * The System V i386 ABI aligns uint64_t to 4 bytes; the x86-64 ABI aligns it to
+ * 8. So `{ uint64_t; uint64_t; uint32_t; }` is 20 bytes when compiled 32-bit and
+ * 24 when compiled 64-bit -- four bytes of tail padding that exist in the READER
+ * and not in the table. coreboot emits 20-byte entries, so a 64-bit consumer of
+ * this header computed `size / 24` for the entry count and strode `map[i]` by 24
+ * over 20-byte records: the first entry read correctly and every one after it was
+ * garbage, which is what "RAM 0 MB" and a device tree with no memory came from.
+ *
+ * coreboot solved this on its own side and says so, in
+ * src/commonlib/include/commonlib/coreboot_tables.h:
+ *
+ *     /-* lb_uint64_t will keep 64bit coreboot table values aligned to 32bit
+ *      * to ensure compatibility. *-/
+ *     typedef __aligned(LB_ENTRY_ALIGN) uint64_t lb_uint64_t;   // LB_ENTRY_ALIGN 4
+ *
+ * This mirrors that typedef rather than reaching for __attribute__((packed)).
+ * Packing would give the same 20 bytes here by coincidence of this struct's
+ * shape; the aligned(4) typedef is the ABI, so it stays right for any other
+ * record that gains a 64-bit field later. Measured: natural 24 / packed 20 /
+ * this 20 on x86-64, and 20 / 20 / 20 on i386. */
+typedef uint64_t lb_uint64_t __attribute__((aligned(4)));
+
 struct lb_memory_range {
-	uint64_t start;
-	uint64_t size;
+	lb_uint64_t start;
+	lb_uint64_t size;
 	uint32_t type;
 #define LB_MEM_RAM       1	/* Memory anyone can use */
 #define LB_MEM_RESERVED  2	/* Don't use this memory region */
